- KEY DATES: ‘LEVIATHAN’ (1651), ENGLISH CIVIL WAR, ‘REFLECTIONS ON THE REVOLUTION IN FRANCE’ (1790)

KEY IDEAS AND POLCIES:-
- KEY IDEAS: PROPERTY, PRAGMATISM, AUTHORITARIANISM, TRADITION, STABILITY AND STATUS QUO 
- POLICIES: MONARCHY, CHURCH OF ENGLAND AND STRONG GOVERNMENT

ONE NATION CONSERVATISM:-

KEY DATES AND FIGURES:-
- Benjamin Disraeli 
- Stanley Baldwin (1923-24, 1924-29) 
- Harold MacMillan (1957-37) 
- Edward Heath (1970- 74)

KEY IDEAS AND POLICIES:-

According to Benjamin Disraeli, traditional conservatism lacked the necessary dynamic to inspire men. He argued for a form of inclusive conservatism – ‘One Nation Conservatism’ 

• ‘One Nation Conservatism’ – helped reach out to the working class. 
• Helped people put their selfish interests below the well- being of the community. 
• Helped the governments of Baldwin, MacMillan and Heath create a prosperous and inclusive society. 

Key Ideas – inclusivity, community, stability and prosperity – all underpinning the traditional conservative values and aiding extensive social reform.

NEW RIGHT:-

KEY DATES:-
- 1979 – Margret Thatcher becomes PM after winning general election. 
- 2nd April 1982 – Falkland wars begin

KEY IDEAS AND POLICIES:-
- Keep tax low ->give people a chance to financially control their own lives 
- Reduce inflation to encourage investments 
- Anti dependency culture -> very expensive 
- Limit unions power -> disrupting markets with excessive pay claims 
- Discourages alternative lifestyle ->threatens traditional family unity 
- Give Government more power to fight crime + stronger defence policy - Emphasize nation state as ultimate source of citizens security. 
- Support NHS 

SUMMARY: Economically: neo-liberal – super small govt intervention in the economy Socially: neo-conservative

CURRENT IDEAS AND POLICIES:-

ECONOMY:- 
- Johnson: big spending, big government approach – levelling up, reminiscent of One Nation conservatism. Public spending was 42% of the economy in 2021, supported by a big rise in corporation tax and national insurance. 
- After Truss’s dramatic and disastrous heavy tax cuts, Sunak has raised taxes and lowered public spending dramatically, similar to Margaret Thatcher’s ‘good house-keeping approach’. 
- Jeremy Hunt: maintained government spending on supporting people with fuel bills, resembling a One Nation approach. Overall, it’s quite a mixed approach at the moment.

WELFARE:-
- Were anti minimum wage in 1997 but in 2015 the Conservative Chancellor of the Exchequer George Osborne, introduced a ‘living wage’ to provide extra support for the least well paid. 
- In 2021, Johnson Created a new secretary for levelling up, housing and communities. 
- Like all Conservatives since Cameron, Truss and Sunak emphasised the Conservative commitment to the National Health Service 
- The Universal Credit system which is based on the principle of the more you earn the less you get suggests an anti-dependency approach more associated with Thatcherism

LAW AND ORDER:-
- Strict enforcement of law + order by recent Conservative governments 
- Investigatory Powers Act 2016 (Theresa May) – protected national security by enhancing government surveillance powers 
- Johnson government – used legislation to increase police powers over public protests (eg Police, Crime, Sentencing + Courts Act) 
- Critics of liberalism as home secretaries – shows uncompromising Thatcherite approach (eg Priti Patel, Suella Braverman) 
- Policing bill went through Royal Assent in 2022, boosting police powers and limiting protes

FOREIGN POLICY:-
- Foreign policy: the Johnson government negotiation of the withdrawal of Britain from the EU (Brexit) resulting in the Tories commitment to keeping the borders strong against illegal migration. In 2020 Boris Johnson announced a £16.5 billion increase in defence spending. 
- This highlights the fact that the Conservative Party are effectively demonstrating how they will enforce their policy about protecting the British interests and that they are determined to “Stop the Boats”

